  "faqSchema": {
    "What is the maximum statutory domestic rental deduction under Section 26RV of the Inland Revenue Ordinance?": "The statutory ceiling is HK$100,000 per year of assessment. The cap is pro-rated if the tenancy covers only part of the fiscal year.",
    "Does a tenancy agreement require formal IRD stamping to qualify for the rental tax deduction?": "Yes. Formal stamping under the Stamp Duty Ordinance is an absolute prerequisite. Unstamped agreements are not accepted by the IRD.",
    "Can a taxpayer claim the rental deduction if their employer reimburses housing costs?": "No. Taxpayers receiving housing benefits or employer rent reimbursement taxed under the 10% rental value rules are ineligible for the Section 26RV deduction.",
    "How is the HK$100,000 ceiling divided if multiple co-tenants share the leased residential flat?": "The HK$100,000 ceiling is apportioned equally among all co-tenants named on the tenancy agreement (e.g., HK$50,000 each for two co-tenants).",
    "Can I claim the rental tax deduction if I or my spouse own a residential property in Hong Kong?": "No. Neither the taxpayer nor their spouse can own any domestic property in Hong Kong during the year of assessment to be eligible.",
    "Can parents claim the rental deduction for domestic premises rented for their student child?": "No. The rented premises must be occupied by the taxpayer as their own principal place of residence."
  }
